   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3ec4e026",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "File Operation- Read And Write Files\n",
    "\n",
    "File handling is a crucial part of any programming language. Python provides built-in functions and methods to\n",
    "read from and write to files, both text and binary. This lesson will cover the basics of file handling, including\n",
    "reading and writing text files and binary files."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "853b2bb1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello , \n",
      "i am mayur patel  \n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# read operations \n",
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",'r') as file :\n",
    "    content = file.read()\n",
    "    print(content)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 30,
   "id": "1017d10d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello world\n",
      "new linen\n",
      "writing with apend\n",
      "writing with apend\n",
      "hello\n",
      "Hello2\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# read a file line by line \n",
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",\"r\") as file :\n",
    "    for line in file :\n",
    "        print(line.strip()) ##strip() remove new line char \n",
    "        \n",
    "        "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "0029125a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# writing file (overwriting)\n",
    "\n",
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",\"w\") as file :\n",
    "    file.write(\"Hello world\\n\") #it overwrite old contain \n",
    "    file.write(\"new linen\\n\")\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 28,
   "id": "c9f558b7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# writing file without overwriting \n",
    "\n",
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",\"a\") as file :\n",
    "    file.write(\"writing with apend\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 31,
   "id": "6e14ae49",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# writing list of lines \n",
    "list1 = [\"hello \\n\",\"Hello2\\n\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",\"a\") as file :\n",
    "    file.writelines(list1)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "466ef54d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The w+ mode in Python is used to open a file for both reading and writing. If the file does not exist, it will be\n",
    "created. If the file exists, its content is truncated (i.e., the file is overwritten)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 32,
   "id": "759cbe8e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hello w+\n",
      "Hello2 w+\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "with open(\"example1.txt\",\"w+\") as file :\n",
    "    file.write(\"Hello w+\\n\")\n",
    "    file.write(\"Hello2 w+\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "    file.seek(0) # to move cursor in first line for reading \n",
    "\n",
    "    contain = file.read()\n",
    "    print(contain)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  }
 ],
